{
 "lead": "Absorption loss is light turned into heat inside the glass. Pure silica absorbs strongly in the ultraviolet and the infrared; this is intrinsic absorption, a property of the glass itself. Impurities, above all the hydroxyl ion OH⁻, add extrinsic absorption bands in between. The two silica tails leave a clear region with its lowest absorption near 1.55 µm, and the OH⁻ band near 1383 nm sits inside it. Only a few syllabi list this topic on its own; they ask for the origins, the wavelengths and sometimes the tail formulas.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Intrinsic absorption: two silica tails",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Intrinsic absorption comes from the SiO₂ network itself, so even perfectly pure glass has it. It has two parts."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The <b>ultraviolet tail</b> is electronic. Photons with enough energy lift electrons across the band gap of silica, and the edge of that absorption trails into the visible and near infrared. It falls as wavelength rises."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The <b>infrared tail</b> is vibrational. The Si–O bond vibrates near 9 µm, and a photon can excite several vibration quanta at once (multiphonon absorption). This tail is tiny at 1.3 µm and rises steeply beyond about 1.6 µm, which is why silica fiber is not used much past 1.65 µm."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For a germania-doped silica core, two empirical formulas give the tails in dB/km, with λ in µm and x the mole fraction of GeO₂:"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>α<sub>UV</sub> = [154.2x/(46.6x + 60)] × 10<sup>−2</sup> e<sup>4.63/λ</sup></b><br>α<sub>UV</sub> is the ultraviolet absorption in dB/km; x is the GeO₂ mole fraction (0.06 means 6 %); λ is the wavelength in µm."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>α<sub>IR</sub> = 7.81 × 10<sup>11</sup> e<sup>−48.48/λ</sup></b><br>α<sub>IR</sub> is the infrared absorption in dB/km; λ is in µm."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Extrinsic absorption: impurities and the water peak",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Extrinsic absorption comes from impurities. Transition-metal ions (Fe²⁺, Cu²⁺, Cr³⁺, Ni²⁺, V³⁺) give broad bands across the visible and near infrared. Vapour-phase preform processes keep them at the parts-per-billion level, so in modern fiber they no longer matter."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The hydroxyl ion OH⁻ remains. Its fundamental O–H stretch vibration lies near 2.73 µm, outside the window, but its overtones fall inside it: near 1.38 µm (the strongest, the <b>water peak</b>), 0.95 µm and 0.72 µm. A combination band of the O–H and Si–O vibrations adds a smaller peak near 1.24 µm."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Two other sources are worth one line each. Atomic defects in the glass, made for example by nuclear radiation, absorb strongly, which matters for fiber in reactors and space. Hydrogen that diffuses into an installed cable forms new OH⁻ and slowly raises the water peak."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The water peak is not a wall. Low-water-peak fiber is drawn from a dried preform and cuts the 1383 nm loss to about the level at 1310 nm. A system on older fiber simply avoids the band and works at 1310 nm or 1550 nm. Some syllabi call the two kinds fundamental and impurity absorption."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "A single-mode fiber has a germania-doped core with x = 0.06. Using the two empirical formulas, find the intrinsic absorption at 1.55 µm and at 1.70 µm, and compare it with a Rayleigh loss of 0.14 dB/km at 1.55 µm.",
   "steps": [
    "At 1.55 µm, UV tail: 154.2 × 0.06 = 9.252 and 46.6 × 0.06 + 60 = 62.796, so the prefactor is (9.252/62.796) × 10<sup>−2</sup> = 1.473 × 10<sup>−3</sup>. With e<sup>4.63/1.55</sup> = e<sup>2.987</sup> = 19.83, α<sub>UV</sub> = 0.029 dB/km.",
    "At 1.55 µm, IR tail: 48.48/1.55 = 31.28 and e<sup>−31.28</sup> = 2.61 × 10<sup>−14</sup>, so α<sub>IR</sub> = 7.81 × 10<sup>11</sup> × 2.61 × 10<sup>−14</sup> = 0.020 dB/km.",
    "Intrinsic total at 1.55 µm: 0.029 + 0.020 = 0.049 dB/km, about a third of the 0.14 dB/km Rayleigh loss.",
    "At 1.70 µm: e<sup>4.63/1.70</sup> = 15.23 gives α<sub>UV</sub> = 0.022 dB/km; e<sup>−48.48/1.70</sup> = 4.12 × 10<sup>−13</sup> gives α<sub>IR</sub> = 0.32 dB/km.",
    "From 1.55 to 1.70 µm the IR term grows sixteenfold, from 0.020 to 0.32 dB/km, while the UV term barely changes."
   ],
   "answer": "Intrinsic absorption is about 0.05 dB/km at 1.55 µm, well below Rayleigh scattering; at 1.70 µm the infrared tail alone is 0.32 dB/km. This is why the low-loss window ends near 1.6 µm."
  },
  {
   "question": "An old cable measures 1.10 dB/km at 1383 nm. A low-water-peak fiber measures 0.26 dB/km at the same wavelength. For a 20 km coarse-WDM channel at 1383 nm, find the extra loss caused by the water peak and the fraction of power it removes.",
   "steps": [
    "Excess coefficient: 1.10 − 0.26 = 0.84 dB/km.",
    "Excess loss over the link: 0.84 dB/km × 20 km = 16.8 dB.",
    "Power fraction kept: 10<sup>−16.8/10</sup> = 10<sup>−1.68</sup> = 0.0209.",
    "Fraction removed: 1 − 0.0209 = 0.979, that is 97.9 %."
   ],
   "answer": "The water peak costs 16.8 dB on this channel and removes 97.9 % of the power that the low-water-peak fiber would deliver."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Putting the water peak at 1550 nm. Its strongest overtone is near 1383 nm; the fundamental is at 2.73 µm.",
  "Calling OH⁻ or metal-ion bands intrinsic. Intrinsic means the silica network itself; impurities are extrinsic.",
  "Treating the whole 1300 to 1500 nm range as unusable. The water peak is a band a few tens of nm wide, and low-water-peak fiber removes most of it.",
  "Blaming absorption for the 1550 nm loss floor. At 1550 nm Rayleigh scattering is the larger part; the infrared tail takes over only beyond about 1.6 µm."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "marks": 2,
   "q": "Name the two intrinsic absorption tails of silica and their origins.",
   "a": "Ultraviolet tail: electronic transitions across the band gap. Infrared tail: Si–O vibrations through multiphonon absorption."
  },
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "marks": 2,
   "q": "Where are the OH⁻ fundamental and its strongest overtone?",
   "a": "Fundamental near 2.73 µm; strongest overtone, the water peak, near 1.38 µm."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "marks": 3,
   "q": "Why does the low-loss window of silica end near 1.6 µm?",
   "a": "The infrared multiphonon tail rises exponentially with wavelength: from about 0.02 dB/km at 1.55 µm to about 0.3 dB/km at 1.7 µm."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "marks": 4,
   "q": "A fiber has 0.6 dB/km of OH⁻ excess loss at 1383 nm. Find the extra loss over 40 km and the percentage of power removed.",
   "a": "A = 0.6 × 40 = 24 dB. Fraction kept 10<sup>−2.4</sup> = 0.00398, so 99.6 % is removed."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "marks": 3,
   "q": "Find α<sub>IR</sub> at 1.60 µm from the empirical formula.",
   "a": "48.48/1.60 = 30.30; e<sup>−30.30</sup> = 6.93 × 10<sup>−14</sup>; α<sub>IR</sub> = 7.81 × 10<sup>11</sup> × 6.93 × 10<sup>−14</sup> = 0.054 dB/km."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "marks": 5,
   "q": "A 1383 nm channel on old fiber has high loss, and moving it to 1310 nm is proposed. Give one gain and one cost.",
   "a": "Gain: the channel leaves the OH⁻ band. Cost: Rayleigh loss is higher at 1310 nm, since it scales as 1/λ⁴ (about 1.24 times the 1383 nm value), and the channel must fit the 1310 nm grid."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "marks": 4,
   "q": "Classify each as intrinsic or extrinsic: OH⁻ overtone, UV electronic tail, Fe²⁺ band, Si–O multiphonon tail.",
   "a": "Extrinsic: OH⁻ overtone, Fe²⁺ band. Intrinsic: UV tail, Si–O multiphonon tail."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Low-water-peak fiber (the ITU-T G.652.D grade) is now the normal choice for access and metro networks, because it opens the 1383 nm band for coarse WDM. Fluoride and chalcogenide glasses push the infrared tail further out for mid-infrared sensing, and hollow-core fibers guide light mostly in air. Search for “G.652.D low water peak” and “multiphonon absorption silica”.",
 "figure": {
  "after_section": 1,
  "caption": "The loss of a germania-doped fiber from the chapter’s formulas: the ultraviolet tail and Rayleigh scattering fall with wavelength, the infrared tail rises steeply past 1.6 µm, and the sum has its minimum, about 0.19 dB/km, near 1.55 µm. The old fiber adds the OH⁻ peak at 1383 nm (1.10 dB/km against 0.26 dB/km).",
